## 5. Verify OCI Infrastructure

Before starting the OKD installation, verify that all OCI resources required by the UPI deployment are available and correctly configured.

### 5.1 Verify Compute Instances

List the Compute instances:

```bash
oci compute instance list \
  --compartment-id <COMPARTMENT_OCID> \
  --query 'data[].{Name:"display-name",State:"lifecycle-state"}'
```

Verify that the required nodes are present:

```text
Bastion
Bootstrap
Master-1
Master-2
Master-3
Worker-1
Worker-2
```

Expected state:

```text
RUNNING
```

---

### 5.2 Verify Private IP Addresses

From the OCI Console, verify that the nodes have the expected private IP addresses.

For this lab:

```text
Bootstrap   → 10.0.2.132
Master-1    → 10.0.2.178
Master-2    → 10.0.2.143
Master-3    → 10.0.2.21
Worker-1    → 10.0.2.81
Worker-2    → 10.0.2.24
```

These addresses should belong to the private subnet:

```text
10.0.2.0/24
```

---

### 5.3 Verify Bastion

Verify that the Bastion is running and has network access to the private subnet.

From your workstation:

```bash
ssh <user>@<bastion-public-ip>
```

On the Bastion:

```bash
ip addr
```

Verify its private address:

```text
10.0.1.173
```

Verify the route to the private subnet:

```bash
ip route
```

---

### 5.4 Verify Private Node Connectivity

From the Bastion, test SSH connectivity to the private nodes:

```bash
ssh <user>@10.0.2.132
ssh <user>@10.0.2.178
ssh <user>@10.0.2.143
ssh <user>@10.0.2.21
ssh <user>@10.0.2.81
ssh <user>@10.0.2.24
```

If the SSH key is available only on your workstation, SSH agent forwarding can be used:

```bash
ssh -A <user>@<bastion-public-ip>
```

Then connect from the Bastion to the private nodes.

---

### 5.5 Verify Load Balancer

Verify that the OCI Load Balancer exists and is active:

```bash
oci lb load-balancer list \
  --compartment-id <COMPARTMENT_OCID> \
  --query 'data[].{Name:"display-name",State:"lifecycle-state",IP:"ip-addresses[0].ip-address"}'
```

For this lab, the Load Balancer public IP was:

```text
130.210.21.43
```

Expected state:

```text
ACTIVE
```

---

### 5.6 Verify Load Balancer Listeners

Verify that the required listeners exist:

```text
6443  → Kubernetes API
22623 → Machine Config Server
80    → HTTP application traffic
443   → HTTPS application traffic
```

These listeners should forward traffic to the appropriate backend nodes.

---

### 5.7 Verify Private DNS

Verify that the OKD DNS records are present and resolve correctly.

From the Bastion:

```bash
dig api.okd.ocp.lab
dig api-int.okd.ocp.lab
dig console-openshift-console.apps.okd.ocp.lab
dig oauth-openshift.apps.okd.ocp.lab
```

The application hostnames should resolve to the OCI Load Balancer IP.

For example:

```text
console-openshift-console.apps.okd.ocp.lab
        │
        ▼
130.210.21.43
```

---

### 5.8 Verify NAT / Outbound Connectivity

Private nodes require outbound connectivity for required installation and cluster operations.

From a private node:

```bash
curl -I https://www.google.com
```

Also verify DNS:

```bash
getent hosts quay.io
```

A successful response confirms that the private node can resolve external names and reach the Internet through the configured OCI network path.

---

### 5.9 Infrastructure Verification Checklist

Before proceeding to the OKD installation:

```text
[ ] All Compute instances are RUNNING
[ ] Bastion is accessible
[ ] Private node IPs are correct
[ ] Bastion can SSH to private nodes
[ ] Load Balancer is ACTIVE
[ ] Required listeners are configured
[ ] DNS records resolve correctly
[ ] Private nodes have outbound connectivity
[ ] Object Storage image is available
[ ] OCI Custom Image is AVAILABLE
```

Once all checks pass, the OCI infrastructure is ready for the **OKD installation configuration and Ignition generation**.